  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The operating environment for trade unions in Namibia is shaped by a relatively strong legal framework that recognises worker rights, but also by structural economic conditions and capacity limitations constrain their reach, influence, and effectiveness, particularly in emerging sectors and vulnerable forms of work.
 
While unions maintain a visible presence in the formal economy and key sectors, their membership density and coverage remain uneven, with more limited reach in small enterprises, informal employment and certain segments and sectors of the economy. As a result, the mobilization and retention of members, along with consistent dues collection, remain ongoing challenges, affecting financial stability and their ability to expand services to members. However, technical and organizational capacities vary across unions, particularly in areas such as leadership and organizational governance systems, evidence-based policy advocacy, sectoral bargaining, labour law and social protection reforms, occupational safety and health (OSH), and engagement in emerging issues and sectors such as responsible business conduct; non-standard employment relations and renewable energies.
 
The ILO supports workers&#x2019; organizations in Namibia to strengthen their institutional, technical and representational capacities to effectively advocate for workers&#x2019; rights and decent work. The interventions focus on improving organizational governance, financial sustainability (increasing membership dues), collective bargaining effectiveness, and policy engagement, alongside strengthening service delivery to members. The strategy includes training, technical assistance, organizational development, and support for participation in social dialogue and labour policy processes. The ILO also supports deepening of their engagement in labour market governance, effectiveness in collective bargaining and dispute resolution, and their role in advancing decent work outcomes across Namibia&#x2019;s key sectors.</narrative>
  </description>
